{"id":"45fedd13-95bd-48b6-b348-690011421e9f","object":"chat.completion","created":1784817050,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['removable']]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    def subtree_ids(node_id, retained_set):\\n        ids = set()\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            nid = stack.pop()\\n            if nid not in retained_set:\\n                continue\\n            ids.add(nid)\\n            for cid in children[nid]:\\n                if cid in retained_set:\\n                    stack.append(cid)\\n        return ids\\n    for cand in candidates:\\n        cid = cand['id']\\n        if cid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if cid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if cand['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(ch in retained for ch in children[cid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        sub = subtree_ids(cid, retained)\\n        sub_symbols = {node_by_id[nid]['symbol'] for nid in sub}\\n        ok = True\\n        for s in required_symbols:\\n            if s not in sub_symbols:\\n                continue\\n            # check if there is at least one retained node with required symbol outside subtree\\n            found_outside = False\\n            for nid in retained:\\n                if nid in sub:\\n                    continue\\n                if node_by_id[nid]['symbol'] == s:\\n                    found_outside = True\\n                    break\\n            if not found_outside:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            retained -= sub\\n            removal_order.append(cid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that performs grammar-guided node reduction. The case contains nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. There is a list of required_symbols. The goal is to reduce the tree by removing nodes (if removable) while preserving required symbols and ensuring that after removal, the tree remains \"grammar-valid\"? Actually the problem mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but there is no grammar provided. Looking at the description: \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we need to simulate that. The task is to reduce a parse tree by deleting nodes (or replacing? but only deletion is mentioned). The constraints: never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children. Only removable nodes can be considered. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use deeper nodes first (depth descending). For equal depth, use node ID as tie break. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order. So we need to output something like: {\"retained_ids\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}. The function must return a JSON-serializable object, so we can return a dict.\n\nWe need to implement a reduction algorithm: start with all nodes, then iteratively consider removable nodes in order (by depth descending, then ID ascending maybe? \"tie break at equal depth\" using node ID, but need to specify order: deeper first, for same depth, stable tie break by node ID. Usually that means sort by depth descending, then by ID ascending for deterministic order. Then try to remove each node if conditions are met: not root, not required symbol, and after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., their nodes are still there). Also, we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained? \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are still in the tree, we cannot remove it (because that would orphan those children?). Actually if we remove a node, its children become orphans; in parse tree reduction, if you remove a node, you also remove its subtree? Or you replace it with a child? The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the constraints say \"Never remove a node with retained children\" which suggests that if a node has children that are still in the tree, we cannot remove it because that would leave children without parent. So we can only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children have already been removed? Actually the process: we consider nodes in order, and when we remove a node, we also remove its entire subtree? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies removing a node means removing that node and perhaps its subtree? But then \"retained children\" might refer to children that are still present. So we can only remove a node if it has no children that are still in the tree (i.e., all its children have already been removed or it's a leaf). So the algorithm is: we start with all nodes. We sort removable nodes by depth descending, then by ID ascending. For each node, check: it is not root, not required symbol, and after removing it (and all its descendants), all required symbols still exist in the set of remaining nodes? But \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means we need to check that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol among the retained nodes after removal. And also check that the node has no children that are still retained? Because if it has children, they would be orphaned. So we can only remove leaf nodes (or nodes whose children are already removed). So we simulate: maintain a set of retained node IDs. Initially all nodes. Then we iterate through candidate nodes in order. For each candidate, check:\n- It is not root (ID \"root\"? Actually root has parent null).\n- Its symbol is not in required_symbols.\n- It has no children that are still retained (i.e., no node in retained set whose parent is this node).\n- If we remove this node (i.e., remove this node and all its descendants? But if we only remove the node itself, its children would be orphaned; so we must remove the entire subtree. So we need to check that after removing the entire subtree rooted at this node, all required symbols are still present. So we need to check that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol outside the subtree.\nIf conditions hold, we remove that node and all its descendants (i.e., remove from retained set). Record the order of removal (the node IDs in order they are removed). Continue. After processing all candidates, we have final retained set. Then return sorted retained IDs and removal order.\n\nBut careful: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if node has retained children, we cannot remove it. That condition is already covered by the check: if any child is retained, then we can't remove. So we skip.\n\nAlso \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We'll sort by -depth (higher depth first), then by node ID (lexicographic? IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. So sort alphabetically).\n\nReturn: {\"retained_ids\": sorted list of IDs of retained nodes, \"removal_order\": list of IDs in order they were removed}.\n\nWe need to build tree structure: map from node ID to node dict, and also from parent to children. Also compute initial depth? Provided. For descendants removal, we need to collect all nodes in subtree. We can do a DFS from node to get all IDs in subtree. But note: some nodes might have already been removed. So we maintain a set of retained IDs. When we remove a node, we remove it and all its retained descendants. So we need to know the subtree structure of the original tree, but only considering nodes still retained. So we can precompute children mapping. Then when we remove a node, we do a DFS from that node, but only including nodes that are still retained. Remove them.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n- Extract payload: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Build children mapping: for each node, add its ID to parent's children list. Also identify root (parent null).\n- Create set retained = set of all node IDs.\n- Create list candidates = [node for node in nodes if node['removable']]. Sort by (-depth, node ID). (Depth is int, ID string)\n- Create list removal_order = [].\n- For each candidate in sorted order:\n   - If candidate ID not in retained: continue (already removed).\n   - Check conditions:\n        - candidate ID != root's ID (root has parent null).\n        - candidate symbol not in required_symbols.\n        - Check any retained child: iterate over children of candidate (from children mapping) and see if any child ID in retained. If yes, skip.\n        - Check required symbols still present after removal: compute subtree nodes (by DFS from candidate, only through retained nodes). Let subtree_ids = set of IDs in that subtree (including candidate). Then for each required symbol, check if there exists any retained node not in subtree_ids that has that symbol. Alternatively, we can precompute symbol counts globally and subtract subtree counts. Simpler: iterate required symbols, check if any node outside subtree has that symbol. We can do: for s in required_symbols, if no node in retained - subtree_ids has symbol s, then fail.\n   - If all conditions pass:\n        - Remove all nodes in subtree_ids from retained.\n        - Append candidate ID to removal_order (maybe also all removed? The spec says \"actual removal order\" - likely just the root of the removed subtree? But the removal of the subtree is triggered by removing the candidate node. So the order should list the candidate node. That's fine.)\n- After processing all candidates, compute retained_ids = sorted list of retained (by ID? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So sort by ID alphabetically).\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nEdge cases: required_symbols includes \"root\"? In fixtures, root is in required_symbols. So we never remove root. Also, some nodes may have non-required symbols but are not removable. They stay.\n\nNote: The algorithm must be deterministic; stable tie break by node ID ensures ordering.\n\nNow, we need to output JSON with key \"implementation\", value is Python source string. We'll write the code as a function. Use only standard library. No file/network/etc.\n\nLet's produce the Python source. It will define `solve(case)` that returns a dict.\n\nWe'll encode as JSON string, escaping properly.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1737,"completion_tokens":2410,"total_tokens":4147,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1853},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":201},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}